    "question": "Những yếu tố nào liên quan đến tiểu đường típ 2?",
    "response": {
      "request_id": "citation-fix-check-1",
      "intent": "knowledge",
      "prediction_result": null,
      "generated_answer": "Tiểu đường típ 2 là dạng tiểu đường phổ biến nhất, xảy ra khi tuyến tụy không sản xuất đủ insulin và cơ thể gặp khó khăn trong việc sử dụng insulin (kháng insulin). [S1]\n\nCác yếu tố nguy cơ phát triển tiểu đường típ 2 bao gồm thừa cân, béo phì, vòng eo lớn, từ 35 tuổi trở lên, có tiền sử gia đình mắc bệnh tiểu đường típ 2, không hoạt động thể chất, hút thuốc, tiền tiểu đường, hoặc có tiền sử tiểu đường thai kỳ hoặc sinh con nặng từ 9 pound trở lên. [S1] [S2]\n\nMột số yếu tố nguy cơ như tuổi tác, tiền sử gia đình hoặc chủng tộc không thể thay đổi, nhưng có thể phòng ngừa hoặc trì hoãn tiểu đường típ 2 bằng cách duy trì cân nặng khỏe mạnh, hoạt động thể chất và bỏ hút thuốc. [S1] [S3]\n\nMô hình học từ khảo sát BRFSS 2015 tại Hoa Kỳ. Lớp 1 gộp tiền tiểu đường và tiểu đường theo nhãn dữ liệu. Điểm mô hình không phải xác suất chẩn đoán hoặc nguy cơ mắc bệnh trong tương lai. Không thay thế xét nghiệm hay bác sĩ.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "citation_id": "S1",
          "document_id": "niddk-symptoms",
          "chunk_id": "51f155be2db8f29c78324d39",
          "title": "NIDDK — Triệu chứng và nguyên nhân",
          "section": "What causes type 2 diabetes?",
          "source_url": "https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/diabetes/overview/symptoms-causes",
          "quote": "Type 2 diabetes is the most common form of diabetes. Type 2 diabetes develops when your pancreas doesn’t produce enough insulin, and your body has trouble using insulin, a condition called insulin resistance. Insulin resistance is when the cells in your muscles, fat, and liver don’t respond well to insulin. When there isn’t enough insulin to balance blood glucose levels, they begin to rise.\n\nWhen blood glucose levels are higher than normal but not high enough to be diagnosed as type 2 diabetes, you have a condition called prediabetes. You have type 2 diabetes when health care professionals diagnose your blood glucose level as being too high.\n\nResearchers don’t fully know what causes problems with insulin production and insulin resistance in type 2 diabetes. However, people are more likely to develop type 2 diabetes if they\n\n- have overweight, obesity, or a large waist size.\n- are age 35 or older. Children and teens can also develop type 2 diabetes, but the risk increases as a person gets older.\n- have a family history of type 2 diabetes.\n- are African American, American Indian, Asian American, Hispanic, Latino, or Pacific Islander.\n- are not physically active, because of a job that requires sitting for long periods of time or because of difficulty moving their body.\n- smoke, including tobacco products and e-cigarettes, or are exposed to secondhand smoke.\n- have prediabetes.\n- have a history of gestational diabetes, a type of diabetes that develops during pregnancy, or gave birth to a baby weighing 9 pounds or more.\n\nYou can’t change some of these risk factors, such as your age, family history, or race. However, you may be able to change some risk factors and prevent or delay type 2 diabetes by maintaining a healthy weight, being physically active, and stopping smoking.\n\nYou may be able to prevent type 2 diabetes by maintaining a healthy weight and being physically active.",
          "source_provider": null,
          "revision_id": null,
          "license": null,
          "license_url": null,
          "attribution_url": null,
          "section_scope": null,
          "changes": null
        },
        {
          "citation_id": "S2",
          "document_id": "niddk-risk",
          "chunk_id": "c185033cc2d2eef7b62ae616",
          "title": "NIDDK — Yếu tố nguy cơ típ 2",
          "section": "What are the risk factors for type 2 diabetes?",
          "source_url": "https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/diabetes/overview/risk-factors-type-2-diabetes",
          "quote": "Diabetes type 2 can affect people at any age.\n\nYou can develop type 2 diabetes at any age, even during childhood. You are more likely to develop type 2 diabetes if you<sup>1</sup>\n\n- are overweight or have obesity.\n- are age 35 or older. Children and teens can also develop type 2 diabetes, but the risk increases as a person gets older.\n- have a family history of diabetes.\n- are African American, American Indian, Asian American, Hispanic/Latino, or Pacific Islander.\n- are not physically active, because of physical limitations, a sedentary lifestyle, or a job that requires sitting for long periods of time.\n- have prediabetes.\n- have a history of gestational diabetes, a type of diabetes that develops during pregnancy, or gave birth to a baby weighing 9 pounds or more.\n\nChildren and teens are also at higher risk of developing type 2 diabetes if, in addition to the previous risk factors, they were born with a low birth weight or if their parent had gestational diabetes while pregnant with the child.<sup>1</sup>\n\nYou are encouraged to talk with a doctor about any of the health conditions listed above that may require medical treatment. Managing health problems may help reduce your chances of developing type 2 diabetes.<sup>2</sup> Also, ask your doctor about any medicines you or your child take that might increase your risk. You can also take the Diabetes Risk Test to learn more about your risk for type 2 diabetes.",
          "source_provider": null,
          "revision_id": null,
          "license": null,
          "license_url": null,
          "attribution_url": null,
          "section_scope": null,
          "changes": null
        },
        {
          "citation_id": "S3",
          "document_id": "niddk-risk",
          "chunk_id": "11b504f8c7ad53f5b5606c37",
          "title": "NIDDK — Yếu tố nguy cơ típ 2",
          "section": "NIDDK — Yếu tố nguy cơ típ 2",
          "source_url": "https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/diabetes/overview/risk-factors-type-2-diabetes",
          "quote": "Your chances of developing type 2 diabetes depend on a combination of risk factors. Although you can’t change risk factors related to family history, age, race, or ethnicity, you may be able to avoid some risk factors by maintaining a healthy weight and being physically active.<sup>1</sup>\n\nRead about risk factors for type 2 diabetes below, and see which ones apply to you. Acting on the factors you can change may help delay or prevent type 2 diabetes.",
